meta {
  name: MCP — tools/call broker:<server>:<tool> (brokered tool, TEMPLATE)
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/mcp
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "jsonrpc": "2.0",
    "id": 4,
    "method": "tools/call",
    "params": {
      "name": "broker:{{mcpBrokerServerName}}:{{mcpBrokerToolName}}",
      "arguments": {}
    }
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 (JSON-RPC envelope, not an HTTP-level failure)", function() {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("fail-closed when no matching downstream server is configured (deterministic TODAY — laptop and v1.24.8 default AUDITTRACE_MCP_BROKER_SERVERS=\"{}\")", function() {
    // This IS a certifiable Rule-1 acceptance criterion, not a
    // placeholder: ADR-063 Phase 2 Track B — "an unrecognised or
    // unconfigured server denies with no forward attempt". The
    // placeholder mcpBrokerServerName/mcpBrokerToolName below never
    // match an operator-configured entry on the laptop OR the v1.24.8
    // candidate deploy, so the broker MUST deny before forwarding —
    // no downstream call is made, no data returned. Neuter that
    // pre-forward check and this test goes RED (see
    // tests/test_mcp_broker.py::TestBrokerToolCall for the same
    // guard, unit-level).
    const result = res.getBody().result;
    expect(result.isError).to.equal(true);
    const payload = JSON.parse(result.content[0].text);
    expect(payload.error).to.include("unknown or disabled downstream server");
  });
}

docs {
  ADR-063 Phase 2 Track B (#296) — the brokered class of `tools/call`.
  This request is a TEMPLATE for the "AuditTrace brokers a real
  downstream MCP server" scenario, which is NOT exercisable at the
  v1.24.8 candidate deploy — the laptop AND that deploy both run the
  portability-invariant default `AUDITTRACE_MCP_BROKER_SERVERS="{}"`
  (no operator-configured downstream). The assertions above are
  therefore scoped to what IS certifiable today: the pre-forward
  deny-and-record path (`services.mcp_broker.broker_tool_call`'s
  "Resolve" + "Authorize" steps) — a well-formed brokered-shaped call
  against an unconfigured server denies cleanly, writes exactly ONE
  `PendingToolCall` (`phase="request"`, no `result_pending`), and never
  reaches the network.

  **To exercise the full brokered path** (forward → two audit rows
  tagged `brokered` → downstream result), an operator must:

  1. Stand up (or point at) a real downstream MCP server exposing at
     least one tool over the same JSON-RPC `tools/call` contract this
     collection uses.
  2. Set `AUDITTRACE_MCP_BROKER_SERVERS` on the deployment to
     `{"<server-name>": {"url": "<downstream /mcp endpoint>",
     "required_scope": "<a scope in the caller's token>"}}`.
  3. Override this request's `mcpBrokerServerName`/`mcpBrokerToolName`
     env vars to match `<server-name>`/an actual tool the downstream
     exposes.
  4. Re-run `01-tools-list.bru` first — the namespaced `broker:*` tool
     should now appear in the manifest.
  5. Re-run this request — expect `result.isError === false`, then
     confirm the two `brokered` audit rows via
     `evidence/03-postgres-tool-calls.bru` (filter on
     `provenance='brokered'`).

  This deferral mirrors the same pattern Track A/B's own build-records
  used for their live E2E (candidate-deploy-gated, looped back to the
  memory server once run) — the .bru file is the deliverable now; the
  live run against a real downstream is a v1.24.8+ exercise.
}
